Phos-Flur Rinse contains sodium fluoride at a concentration of 0.044% (w/v). How many milligrams of sodium fluoride are present in a 10 mL dose?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
Phos-Flur Rinse contains sodium fluoride at a concentration of 0.044% (w/v). How many milligrams of sodium fluoride are present in a 10 mL dose?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the mass of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Phos-Fol rinse, we can use the given concentration (0.044% w/v) and the volume of the dose (10 mL).

The concentration (0.044% w/v) means that there are 0.044 grams of sodium fluoride in 100 mL of Phos-Fol rinse.

First, we can find the mass of sodium fluoride in 10 mL of Phos-Fol rinse:
\[
\text{Mass of sodium fluoride} = \frac{0.044 \text{ g}}{100 \text{ mL}} \times 10 \text{ mL} = 0.0044 \text{ g}
\]

Now, we need to convert the mass from grams to milligrams. Since 1 g = 1000 mg, we can multiply the mass in grams by 1000 to get the mass in milligrams:
\[
\text{Mass of sodium fluoride} = 0.0044 \text{ g} \times 1000 \text{ mg/g} = 4.4 \text{ mg}
\]

So, there are 4.4 milligrams of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Phos-Fol rinse.

Final answer: \(\boxed{4.4 \text{ mg}}\)",relevance,"# Task Description
