","Stem hole borer (SBB) is a major threat to coffee production in many regions. These small fungi-feeding birds bore holes into the stems of the plant, leading to stunted growth, reduced yield, and even death. Controlling them can be challenging, but here are some recommended methods to manage SBB infections in coffee plants:

1. Prevention: Prevention is key to minimizing SBB damage.

   a. Choose Healthy Seed and Seedlings: Start with healthy, healthy plants with no SBB symptoms.

   b. Weed Management: Remove weeds and other weeds that can serve as hosts for SBB.

   c. Proper Irrigation: Ensure proper watering to prevent stress on the plants, making them more susceptible to SBB.

   c. Proper Pest Management: Monitor for other pests and diseases that can stress the plants and make them more susceptible to SBB.

2. Cultural Practices: These methods help reduce the risk of SBB and reduce their impact on the crop.

   a. Pruning: Remove and discard (not composted!) infected stems and stems with holes. This helps prevent the spread of fungal pathogens carried by the birds. Pruning should be done during the dormant stage (e.g., after harvest).

   b. Pruning and Pruning: Proper pruning can help reduce competition between plants and improve air circulation, which can help control SBB.

   c. Pruning and Pruning: Proper pruning can help reduce competition between plants and improve air circulation, which can help control SBB.

   c. Soil Improvement: Improve soil health with organic matter and proper pH levels to support healthy growth and reduce susceptibility to SBB.

