","The correct answer is $\boxed{(c) \text{ZnO}}$.  

**Explanation:**  
- The reduction of metal oxides by carbon is determined by the relative reduction potentials of the metal and carbon.  
- The general reaction is:  
  \[ \text{MO} + C \rightarrow M + CO \]  
- For this reaction to occur, the reduction potential of the metal (M) must be lower than that of carbon (C).  
- The reduction potentials of the given metal oxides are:  
  - \( \text{Al}_2\text{O}_3 \): \( \text{Al} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}^{2+} \) (Reduction potential is low, can be reduced by carbon)  
  - \( \text{PbO} \): \( \text{Pb} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb}^{2+} \) (Reduction potential is low, can be reduced by carbon)  
  - \( \text{ZnO} \): \( \text{Zn} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+} \) (Reduction potential is higher than that of carbon, cannot be reduced by carbon)  
  - \( \text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 \): \( \text{Fe} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+} \) (Reduction potential is low, can be reduced by carbon)  

- Thus, \( \text{ZnO} \) cannot be reduced to metal by carbon.  

**Note:**  
- The reduction of \( \text{ZnO} \) by carbon is not feasible because \( \text{Zn} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+} \) is less favorable than \( \text{C} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{C}_2^- \).  

