","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the point-point form of the equation of a line:

\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]

This equation is derived as follows:
1. The point \( (a, f(a)) \) is on the line, so the equation must satisfy this point.
2. The slope of the tangent line at \( (a, f(a)) \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( a \), which is \( f'(a) \).
3. Using the point-point form of the line equation, with the point \( (a, f(a)) \) and slope \( f'(a) \), we get:
   \[
   y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
   \]

Thus, the equation of the tangent line is:
\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]

This is the equation of the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at \( (a, f(a)) \).",fluency,"# Task Description
