- The kinetic molecular theory of gases is based on the following postulates:  
  - Gases are composed of individual particles (atoms or molecules).  
  - These particles are in constant random motion.  
  - The particles do not have a definite volume (they occupy a large volume).  
  - The particles are in no distances from each other (they are far apart).  
  - The particles do not exert any forces on each other (no intermolecular forces).  
  - The average kinetic energy of the particles is directly proportional to the temperature of the gas.  

- Option (d) is incorrect because the kinetic molecular theory does not state that gases are purely monatomic (composed of individual atoms). Gases can be monatomic (like helium) or polyatomic (composed of molecules like nitrogen or water).  

