</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy is estimated using the dislocation theory, specifically the intercepting of dislocations by the hard particles. The dislocation theory is given by the equation:

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{b^2 G}{2a}
\]

where:
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector
- \(G\) is the shear modulus
- \(a\) is the average center-to-center spacing of the particles.

Substituting the given values:
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\)
- \(a = 6 \times 10^{-8}\) m

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{(0.286 \times 10^{-9})^2 \times 26 \times 10^9}{2 \times 6 \times 10^{-8}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{8.1 \times 10^{-19} \times 26 \times 10^9}{1.2 \times 10^{-7}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{210.6 \times 10^{-10}}{1.2 \times 10^{-7}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = 1.755 \times 10^{-2} \times 10^7
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = 175.5 \text{ MPa}
\]

Thus, the estimated contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{175.5 \text{ MPa}}\).
