\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \hbar
\]  
For a unstable particle, the energy difference is the energy of the decay process, and the time is the lifetime of the particle.  

The uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) is related to the energy of the decay process, which is inversely proportional to the lifetime (\(\tau\)):  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{\tau}
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]  
Convert this energy to MeV:  
\[
\Delta E \approx 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.66 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} \approx 31.8 \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the uncertainty in the rest energy is approximately 31.8 MeV.  

The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:  
\[
1385 \pm 31.8 \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the range is:  
\[
1353.2 \text{ MeV} \leq \text{Measured energy} \leq 1416.8 \text{ MeV}
\]  

