","The decay length (\(L\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
L = \frac{1}{k_0 (n_{\text{eff}} - n_f)}
\]  
where:  
- \(k_0\) is the free-index component of the wavelength (\(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\)),  
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the waveguide,  
- \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the surrounding medium.  

For this case, the decay length is given as \(L = \frac{1}{k_0 (n_{\text{eff}} - n_f)}\), and \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\). The wavelength is \(1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}\).  

1. **Calculate \(k_0\):**  
   \[
   k_0 = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} = \frac{2\pi}{1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}} = 6.28 \times 10^6 \text{ rad/m}
   \]  

2. **Substitute into the decay length formula:**  
   \[
   L = \frac{1}{6.28 \times 10^6 \text{ rad/m} \times 0.3} = \frac{1}{1.886 \times 10^6 \text{ rad/m}} \approx 5.3 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}
   \]  

