An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The open gain is \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback ratio is \( m_v = 0.1 \). The gain decreases by 6 dB due to ageing.

The closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11
\]

The gain \( A_v \) decreases by 6 dB, which means the new gain is:
\[
A_v' = 100 \times 10^{-6/10} = 100 \times 0.1 = 10
\]

The new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{10}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{10}{0.9} \approx 11.11
\]

The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \times 100 = \frac{11.11 - 111.11}{111.11} \times 100 \approx \frac{-100}{111.11} \times 100 \approx -89.99
\]

Rounding to the nearest whole number, the percentage change is \(\boxed{-90}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
