## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The time dilation formula states that the time \( t \) in a moving frame (e.g., the spaceship) is related to the time \( t_0 \) in a stationary frame (e.g., Earth) by:
\[
t = t_0 \sqrt{1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}}
\]
where \( v \) is the relative velocity between the two frames and \( c \) is the speed of light.

In this case, the clock in the cardboard is the same as the clock in the cardboard, so the time dilation is the same for both. The time in the cardboard is:
\[
t = 3 \sqrt{1 - \0.6^2} = 3 \sqrt{1 - 0.36} = 3 \sqrt{0.64} = 3 \times 0.8 = 2.4 \text{ years}
\]
Thus, the time observed on the cardboard is 2.4 years. 

The correct answer is \(\boxed{2.4 \text{ years}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

